\section{testthat's teardown environment}{

Like in \code{testthat::test_dir()}, what the \verb{helper-*.R} and \verb{setup-*.R} files, steps and hooks
defer to \code{testthat::teardown_env()}, like \code{withr::defer(DBI::dbDisconnect(con), teardown_env())},
runs when \code{test()} ends, also when it's called in a testthat test file.
}

\section{test() in a testthat file}{

\code{test()} keeps the step definitions, hooks and parameter types registered before it, like by
the setup files of the testthat run, for the test files after it. A setup file of \code{path} that
testthat runs too, like with \code{test(".")} in a file of \code{tests/testthat}, defines its step
definitions again rather than twice. \code{test(".")} in a file of \code{tests/testthat} tells testthat
which snapshot files its feature files use, and is an error in a parallel testthat run.
}

\section{Good Practices}{

\itemize{
\item Use a separate directory for your acceptance tests, e.g. \code{tests/acceptance}.

It's not prohibited to use \code{tests/testthat} directory, but it's not recommended as those tests
serve a different purpose and are better run separately, especially if acceptance tests take
longer to run than unit tests.

If you want to run Cucumber tests alongside \code{testthat} tests, you can use \code{cucumber::run()}
in one of the \verb{test-*.R} files in your \code{tests/testthat} directory.
\item Use \verb{test-*.R} files to test the support code you might have implemented that is used to run Cucumber tests.

Those tests won't be run when calling \code{\link[=test]{test()}}. To run those tests use
\code{testthat::test_dir("tests/acceptance")}.
}
}

